### Description

`calculateWorkflowChecksum()` in `packages/workflow/src/workflow-checksum.ts` builds the SHA-256 payload by copying every field listed in `WORKFLOW_CHECKSUM_FIELDS` unless the value is `undefined`:

```
for (const field of WORKFLOW_CHECKSUM_FIELDS) {
	const value = workflow[field];
	if (value !== undefined) {
		checksumPayload[field] = value;
	}
}
```

Two of those fields are declared as nullable in `WorkflowSnapshot`: `description?: string | null` and `activeVersionId?: string | null`. A field that is absent (`undefined`) is skipped, but the same field set explicitly to `null` is copied into the payload and serialized as `"description":null` (or `"activeVersionId":null`). Two snapshots with otherwise identical content therefore produce different hashes.

The function is documented as calculating a checksum of workflow content fields for conflict detection. With the current logic, a snapshot where a nullable field is `null` and a snapshot where that field is simply missing are seen as different content, so conflict detection reports a difference even though the workflow itself has not changed.

This is inconsistent with the rest of the package. `normalizeNodeShape()` in `packages/workflow/src/schemas.ts` is documented with "Top-level keys that are `.optional()` but not `.nullable()` — `null` must be omitted." and deletes those keys. The existing test `should generate same checksum when a setting is undefined i.e. missing` in `packages/workflow/test/workflow-checksum.test.ts` states "undefined fields should be ignored, so checksums should be the same". A nullable top-level field should be normalized the same way.

### To Reproduce

Compare two snapshots that differ only in how the absent description is represented:

```
const missing = { name: 'Sync', nodes: [], connections: {} };
const cleared = { name: 'Sync', nodes: [], connections: {}, description: null };

await calculateWorkflowChecksum(missing);
await calculateWorkflowChecksum(cleared);
```

Actual result: the two calls return different 64-character hex hashes, because `cleared` includes `"description":null` in the serialized payload while `missing` does not.

The same happens for `activeVersionId: null`.

### Expected behavior

Both calls should return the same hash, because a `null` description or `activeVersionId` represents the same "no description / no active version" content as an absent field. A real value (for example `description: 'Nightly sync'` or `activeVersionId: 'v-42'`) must still change the checksum, and a nested `null` inside `meta` or `settings` must remain significant.

### Notes

Only the top-level fields iterated by `calculateWorkflowChecksum()` are affected; the recursive key sorting performed by `sortObjectKeys()` is unchanged.

